## Shell scripting

Shell scripting is the process of writing a series of commands inside a text file that is executed by a command-line interpreter, known as the shell.


Key Concepts
Shell: An interface that interprets human-readable commands and translates them into instructions the operating system's kernel can execute.
Script: A plain text file containing a sequence of commands, variables, control structures, and comments.
Shebang: The first line of a shell script, starting with #! followed by the path to the interpreter (e.g., #!/bin/bash), which tells the system which shell to use to run the script.
Execution: Before running a script, you usually need to grant it execution permissions using the chmod +x scriptname.sh command. 

Primary use - 

> Task Automation
> System Monitoring
> Batch Processing
> Environment Setup & Deployment
